<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Derive the quadratic formula — fill in the missing steps</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Work through the derivation of the quadratic formula for \(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\) (with \(a \neq 0\)) by completing the square. Fill in each missing step, giving exact expressions in terms of \(a\), \(b\) and \(c\).</p>
<p>Dividing every term by \(a\) gives \[ x^2 + \frac{b}{a}x + \frac{c}{a} = 0. \]</p>
<p><strong>Step 1.</strong> Move the constant term to the right-hand side.</p>
<div>\( x^2 + \dfrac{b}{a}x = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p><strong>Step 2.</strong> To complete the square, which term must be added to <em>both</em> sides?</p>
<div>Add [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<p><strong>Step 3.</strong> The left-hand side is now \(\left(x+\dfrac{b}{2a}\right)^2\). Write the right-hand side as a single fraction with denominator \(4a^2\).</p>
<div>\( \left(x+\dfrac{b}{2a}\right)^2 = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
<p><strong>Step 4.</strong> Take square roots and solve for \(x\). Give the two solutions as a set. Type <code>sqrt(b^2-4*a*c)</code> for \(\sqrt{b^2-4ac}\).</p>
<div>\( x \in \) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The quadratic formula is what you get when you <strong>complete the square</strong> on the general equation \(ax^2+bx+c=0\). Completing the square rewrites \(x^2 + px\) as \(\left(x+\frac{p}{2}\right)^2 - \left(\frac{p}{2}\right)^2\); here \(p = \dfrac{b}{a}\), so half of it is \(\dfrac{b}{2a}\).</p>
\[ \begin{align*}
ax^2 + bx + c &= 0\\
x^2 + \dfrac{b}{a}x + \dfrac{c}{a} &= 0 &&\text{divide by } a\\
x^2 + \dfrac{b}{a}x &= -\dfrac{c}{a} &&\text{Step 1: move the constant}\\
x^2 + \dfrac{b}{a}x + \dfrac{b^2}{4a^2} &= \dfrac{b^2}{4a^2} - \dfrac{c}{a} &&\text{Step 2: add } \left(\dfrac{b}{2a}\right)^2\\
\left(x+\dfrac{b}{2a}\right)^2 &= \dfrac{b^2-4ac}{4a^2} &&\text{Step 3: } \dfrac{c}{a} = \dfrac{4ac}{4a^2}\\
x + \dfrac{b}{2a} &= \pm\dfrac{\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} &&\text{square root of both sides}\\
x &= \dfrac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} &&\text{Step 4}
\end{align*} \]
<p>The two solutions are \(\dfrac{-b+\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\) and \(\dfrac{-b-\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
